{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And He said, 'Cast it to the ground,' and he cast it to the ground.</b> I do not understand why He performed the signs for Moses, for Moses believed that HaShem was speaking with him. Rather, it would seem that He should have said: the staff that is in your hand, you shall cast to the ground before them, and it shall become a serpent — and likewise with the second sign, as He said with the third: 'And you shall take of the waters of the river' (Exodus 4:9). Therefore the words of our Sages (Shemot Rabbah 3:16) are well-founded, that the first sign was an allusion that he had spoken against them evil speech, and the second to punish him with it. And this is the meaning of <b>and Moses fled from before it</b>, for he feared lest he be punished and the serpent bite him — and every person recoils from what harms him, even though he knows that if it be so by the will of HaShem, none can deliver from His hand. <b>Or perhaps</b>, even though HaShem had informed him (above, verses 14–15) of the great Name by which the world was created and through which all things came into being, He wished to show him that through it signs and wonders would be performed by changing the natures of created things, in order that the matter be strengthened in Moses' heart and he know in truth that through his hand renewed things would be done in the world. And two signs were sufficient for Moses, and there was no water there, therefore He commanded him to perform the third sign before the eyes of the people."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:02:30.913809566Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}